FRM.registerQuestions(44, [{"id":"918","page":425,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Adjustable-rate mortgage loans."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Non-agency loans."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Jumbos."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"Agency loans."}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Mortgage-backed loans played a significant role in the 2007-2009 financial crisis. After the crisis, the importance of securitization of these loans increased furthermore. In the United States, there are multiple entities that securitize mortgage loans. Which of the following types of loans is securitized through the Federal National Mortgage Association?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Agency loans, also known as conforming loans, are typically residential loans that are securitized through entities like the Federal National Mortgage Association (FNMA), Government National Mortgage Association (GNMA), and Federal Home Loan Mortgage Corporation (FHLMC). These loans conform to the guidelines set by these government-sponsored entities (GSEs). The GSEs buy these loans from lenders, package them into mortgage-backed securities (MBS), and guarantee the timely payment of principal and interest to the MBS investors. This process helps to provide liquidity to the mortgage market, enabling lenders to make more loans. The FNMA, commonly known as Fannie Mae, is one of the leading agencies in this process."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice A is incorrect. Adjustable-rate mortgage loans are not typically securitized through the Federal National Mortgage Association (FNMA). While FNMA does deal with adjustable-rate mortgages, it is not the primary type of loan they securitize."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice B is incorrect. Non-agency loans are those that are not backed by government agencies. The Federal National Mortgage Association, being a government-sponsored enterprise, primarily deals with agency loans and does not typically securitize non-agency loans."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice C is incorrect. Jumbos or jumbo loans exceed the conforming loan limits set by the Federal Housing Finance Agency (FHFA), and hence, they are not usually securitized through FNMA which primarily deals with conforming loans within the set limits."}],"remember":["Securitization is the process of pooling various types of debt, such as mortgages, auto loans, or credit card debt, and selling them as securities to investors.","Government-sponsored entities (GSEs) like FNMA, GNMA, and FHLMC play a crucial role in the securitization of mortgage loans by providing liquidity to the market.","Conforming loans are those that meet the guidelines set by GSEs and are typically easier to securitize compared to non-conforming loans.","Mortgage-backed securities (MBS) are financial products that are created by pooling together various mortgage loans and selling them to investors.","The 2007-2009 financial crisis was triggered in part by the collapse of the subprime mortgage market, which had a significant impact on the securitization of mortgage loans."]},{"id":"919","page":427,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"$1,004.61"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"$923.94"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"$763.84"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"$340.60"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Mohan Singh, a sales manager at a retail chain, has recently moved to Minnesota with his wife. He purchased a studio apartment with a mortgage loan of $140,000 at 5% for 20 years. Which of the following is the most appropriate estimation of the monthly installments on this loan?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The monthly installments of $923.93 for the monthly periods of 20 years (12x20=240) at the rate of 5% results in the present value of the loan that is $140,000."},{"type":"p","text":"The easiest way to solve this problem is with the help of the financial calculator:"},{"type":"p","text":"N=20×12=240; I/Y=5/12=0.4167; PV=140,000; FV=0; CPT => PMT=-923.94"},{"type":"p","text":"This installment can also be calculated with the simple annuity formula as follows:"},{"type":"p","text":"P V = installment [1 − (1 + r)−n]"},{"type":"pre","text":"r"},{"type":"p","text":"Since the annual rate of interest is 5%, the monthly rate can be approximated as 5%/12 = 0.004167 or 0.4167%"},{"type":"pre","text":"[1−1.004167−240 ]"},{"type":"p","text":"Thus, 140, 000 = installment 0.004167"},{"type":"pre","text":"Installment = 140,000 = $923.94\n                      { [1−01.0.00044116677−24}0]"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"920","page":427,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"It allows the lender to demand the repayment before the maturity of the loan."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"It restricts the borrower from repaying the mortgage before the maturity date."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"It allows the borrower to demand the disbursement of the mortgage loan before the agreed disbursement date."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"It allows the borrower to repay the mortgage loan before the maturity date of the loan."}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Which of the following is the accurate explanation of a mortgage loan with a prepayment option?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"A mortgage loan with a prepayment option indeed allows the borrower to repay the mortgage loan before the maturity date of the loan. This option provides the borrower with the flexibility to manage their finances more effectively. If the borrower comes into a financial windfall or manages to save enough money, they can choose to repay the loan early, thereby saving on future interest payments. This option is particularly beneficial in a declining interest rate environment. If interest rates decline, the present value of future payments will be higher. Hence, the borrower can choose to retire the mortgage early, thereby saving on future interest payments. However, it's important to note that some lenders may charge a prepayment penalty to compensate for the loss of interest income. Therefore, borrowers should carefully read the loan agreement before deciding to prepay their mortgage."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice A is incorrect. This statement is not accurate because the prepayment option in a mortgage loan does not allow the lender to demand repayment before the maturity of the loan. Instead, it gives this right to the borrower."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice B is incorrect. This statement contradicts with what a prepayment option offers. The prepayment option does not restrict but allows borrowers to repay their mortgage loans before their maturity date."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice C is incorrect. The disbursement of a mortgage loan before its agreed date has nothing to do with a prepayment option. Prepayment refers specifically to repaying part or all of a debt obligation earlier than its due date, and not about demanding disbursement earlier."}],"remember":["Prepayment option in a mortgage loan allows the borrower to repay the loan before the maturity date, providing flexibility and potential interest savings.","In a declining interest rate environment, prepaying a mortgage can be advantageous as it helps in saving on future interest payments.","Some lenders may charge a prepayment penalty to compensate for the loss of interest income when borrowers choose to prepay their mortgage.","It's essential for borrowers to carefully review the loan agreement to understand any prepayment terms and conditions before deciding to prepay their mortgage."]},{"id":"921","page":429,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"The use of the prepayment option is appropriate if the mortgage rate increases to 3.75%."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"The use of the prepayment option is appropriate if the mortgage rate remains at 3.25%."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"The use of the prepayment option is appropriate if the mortgage decreases to 3.0%."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"None of the above; prepayment is not a function of interest rates."}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Alison Garry and her partner have recently secured a mortgage to buy a condominium in a beach town in Costa Rica. The purchase price of the condo was $40,000, and the mortgage has an interest rate of 3.25% for a period of 10 years. The mortgage also includes a prepayment option. In light of these circumstances, which of the following statements would be the most accurate regarding the appropriate use of the prepayment option?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"A prepayment option mortgage loan allows the mortgage borrower to pay back the outstanding principal before its predefined maturity date. A mortgage borrower can benefit from the prepayment option if the interest rates decline. The prepayment option is valuable for the borrower when the mortgage rates decline. As the rates will decline, the present value of the remaining monthly payments will be greater than the principal outstanding. Hence, the borrower can gain by paying the principal outstanding in exchange for not having to make further mortgage payments. Think of this along the lines of refinancing. If rates fall, you can pay off the existing mortgage and take on a new one that will come with lower coupon payments."}],"remember":["Prepayment option allows the borrower to pay off the outstanding principal before the maturity date of the loan.","Prepayment option is beneficial for the borrower when interest rates decrease.","Refinancing is a common strategy used by borrowers to take advantage of lower interest rates.","Prepayment option can help borrowers save on interest costs over the life of the loan."]},{"id":"922","page":429,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Securitization is the process of securing the mortgage with a security or collateral, which the lender can use in case of default."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Securitization is the process of setting a bankruptcy-remote entity with the sole purpose issuing bank loans to individual borrowers."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Securitization is the process of setting a bankruptcy-remote entity with the sole purpose of acquiring asset-backed securities (ABSs)."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"Securitization is the process of converting a group of nonmarketable assets, or expected future cash flows on the assets, into units of marketable securities."}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Ahmed Saeed has recently graduated from the Frankfurt Finance School with a Bachelor’s degree. He was invited by a small-size audit firm that provides audit services to small-medium companies and startups to take a test in order to join the firm as a junior risk analyst. In the test, he was asked to identify the definition of securitization. Which of the following is the appropriate definition of securitization?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Securitization is indeed the process of converting a group of nonmarketable assets, or expected future cash flows on the assets, into units of marketable securities. This process is often carried out by financial institutions, such as banks, to transfer the risk associated with these assets to the market. By doing so, these institutions are able to generate funds for their business operations. The securitization process involves the creation of a special purpose vehicle (SPV) or special purpose entity (SPE), which purchases the assets from the originator. The SPV/SPE then issues securities that are backed by these assets, which are sold to investors. The cash flows generated by the underlying assets are used to service the securities. This process allows the originator to remove these assets from their balance sheet, thereby improving their financial position."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice A is incorrect. Securitization does not involve securing the mortgage with a security or collateral. This definition is more aligned with the concept of a secured loan, where the borrower pledges an asset as collateral for the loan."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice B is incorrect. While securitization does involve setting up a bankruptcy-remote entity, its purpose isn't to issue bank loans to individual borrowers. Rather, this entity's purpose is to hold assets and issue securities backed by these assets."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice C is incorrect. The statement incorrectly defines securitization as setting up an entity for acquiring asset-backed securities (ABSs). In reality, securitization involves creating ABSs from non-marketable assets or expected future cash flows on these assets."}],"remember":["Securitization helps financial institutions to transfer risk associated with assets to the market.","Special Purpose Vehicles (SPVs) or Special Purpose Entities (SPEs) are created in the securitization process to purchase assets from the originator.","The cash flows from the underlying assets are used to service the securities issued in securitization.","Securitization allows originators to improve their financial position by removing assets from their balance sheet.","Securitization is a key mechanism for liquidity management and risk transfer in financial markets."]},{"id":"924","page":431,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.0302"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.1243"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.132"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.1402"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Billy Clark is an investment manager at the Sachsenhausen Investment Bank based in Dusseldorf. Clark manages a pool of mortgages and the assets constructed with the pool. If the pool prepaid 1.1% of its principal above its amortizing principal as the percentage of total outstanding principal in the month of February, then which of the following is the appropriate annualized constant prepayment rate he can come up with?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"If the pool prepaid 1.1% of its principal above its amortizing principal as the percentage of total outstanding principal in the month, then its single monthly mortality rate or SMM is 1.1%."},{"type":"pre","text":"Constant prepayment rate or CPR = 1 − (1 − SMM)12\n                                             CP R = 1 − (1 − 0.011)12 = 0.1243"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"926","page":431,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Prime fixed-rate MBS."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Subprime fixed-rate MBS."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Prime adjustable-rate mortgage."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"Subprime adjustable-rate mortgage."}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Pooja Rao has recently joined Green Oceans Hedge Fund that invests in non-conventional investment assets and securities. Rao was instructed by the fund manager to evaluate four mortgage-backed securities of four different banks, and identify the riskiest MBS. Identify for Rao which of these MBSs has the highest risk."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Subprime adjustable-rate mortgages (ARMs) are considered to be the riskiest type of mortgage-backed securities (MBSs). This is primarily due to the nature of the borrowers and the structure of the loans. Subprime mortgages are typically extended to borrowers who have poor creditworthiness, which is often reflected in higher loan-to-value (LTV) and debt-to-income (DTI) ratios. These borrowers are more likely to default on their loans, making the securities backed by these mortgages riskier. Additionally, the adjustable-rate structure of these mortgages adds another layer of risk. Unlike fixed-rate mortgages, ARMs have interest rates that can fluctuate over time. If interest rates rise, the borrowers' mortgage payments can increase significantly, further increasing the likelihood of default. Therefore, among the four options, subprime ARMs are the riskiest MBSs."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice A is incorrect. Prime fixed-rate MBSs are generally considered to be less risky than their subprime counterparts. This is because prime borrowers have a lower risk of defaulting on their loans, which reduces the overall risk of the MBS. Additionally, the fixed rate nature of these securities means that they are not subject to interest rate fluctuations, further reducing their risk profile."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice B is incorrect. Subprime fixed-rate MBSs carry more risk than prime fixed-rate MBSs due to the higher likelihood of default by subprime borrowers. However, they still have a lower level of risk compared to adjustable-rate mortgages (ARMs), as ARMs expose investors to additional interest rate risks."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice C is incorrect. While prime adjustable-rate mortgages do carry some additional interest rate risks compared with fixed-rate mortgages, they are still less risky than subprime ARMs due to the lower default rates associated with prime borrowers."}],"remember":["Mortgage-Backed Securities (MBS): MBS are investment products that are backed by a pool of mortgages. Investors in MBS receive payments based on the interest and principal payments made by borrowers on the underlying mortgages.","Fixed-Rate Mortgages: Mortgages with a fixed interest rate that remains constant throughout the life of the loan.","Adjustable-Rate Mortgages (ARMs): Mortgages with an interest rate that can change periodically based on changes in a specified index.","Loan-to-Value (LTV) Ratio: A ratio used to assess the risk of a mortgage loan by comparing the loan amount to the appraised value of the property.","Debt-to-Income (DTI) Ratio: A ratio used to evaluate a borrower's ability to manage monthly payments and repay debts by comparing total monthly debt payments to gross monthly income.","Default Risk: The risk that a borrower will be unable to make required payments on a loan, leading to potential losses for investors in MBS.","Interest Rate Risk: The risk that changes in interest rates will affect the value of fixed-income securities, such as MBS."]},{"id":"928","page":433,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"$94,471.22"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"$73,687.56"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"$45,750"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"$34,890.74"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Boris Arkarov is a Russian real estate investor based in California. He has been investing in the real estate sector of California for the past 20 years and is famous for selling some luxurious villas to well-known Hollywood celebrities. In a magazine interview, Mr. Arkarov expressed that he wants to borrow to finance his personal residential estate in the suburbs of L.A that costs $10.5 million. If the loan-to-value ratio is 78%, calculate the amount of monthly payment that Mr. Arkarov has to pay on a 20-year mortgage at the rate of 9%."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"1. Calculate the Loan Amount."},{"type":"pre","text":"Loan Amount = Property Value × Loan-to-Value Ratio\n                     = $10, 500, 000 × 0.78\n                     = $8,190,000"},{"type":"p","text":"2. Calculate the Monthly Interest Rate."},{"type":"pre","text":"        Annual Interest Rate\nMonthly  Interest Rate =                      Annual Interest  Rate\n                              =               0.09 12\n                                              12\n                                            = 0.0075"},{"type":"p","text":"3. Calculate the Total Number of Payments."},{"type":"p","text":"Total Payments = Loan Term in Years × 12"},{"type":"pre","text":"= 20 × 12\n= 240"},{"type":"p","text":"4. Calculate the Monthly Payment."},{"type":"pre","text":"P                         =  1  r ×PV\n                                − (1 + r)−n\n                             0.0075 × $8,190,000\n                          = 1 − (1 + 0.0075)−240\n                          ≈ $73, 687.56"},{"type":"p","text":"So, with a loan amount of $8,190,000, a monthly interest rate of 0.0075, and 240 total payments, the monthly mortgage payment comes out to approximately $73,687.56."}],"remember":["Loan-to-Value Ratio (LTV): The ratio of the loan amount to the appraised value or purchase price of the property.","Monthly Interest Rate: The annual interest rate divided by 12 to get the monthly rate.","Loan Term: The number of years over which the loan will be repaid.","Amortization: The process of paying off a debt over time through regular payments.","Mortgage Payment Formula: The formula to calculate the monthly mortgage payment based on the loan amount, interest rate, and loan term."]},{"id":"933","page":434,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Contraction risk is the risk related to the increase in the expected life of a mortgage pool due to falling interest rates and higher prepayment rates."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Contraction risk is the risk related to the increase in the expected life of a mortgage pool due to increasing interest rates and lower prepayment rates."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Contraction risk is the risk related to the decrease in the expected life of a mortgage pool due to falling interest rates and higher prepayment rates."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"Contraction risk is the risk related to the decrease in the expected life of a mortgage pool due to increasing interest rates and lower prepayment rates."}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Anna Henderson is a high net-worth individual investor with Galaxy Investments Inc. Anna has recently learned about the investments and returns of mortgage-backed securities. Jacob Glen, a dedicated investment manager, briefed Henderson that she does not need to concern about the contraction risk of mortgage pool as their investment products are designed to mitigate risk. Which of the following is the most appropriate explanation for the contraction risk?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Contraction risk in the context of mortgage-backed securities refers to the risk associated with the decrease in the expected life of a mortgage pool. This decrease is typically triggered by higher prepayment rates, which are often a result of falling interest rates. When interest rates fall, borrowers are more likely to refinance their mortgages at these lower rates, leading to a higher rate of prepayments. This, in turn, reduces the expected life of the mortgage pool, hence the term 'contraction risk'. This risk is a significant concern for investors in mortgage-backed securities as it can impact the timing and amount of their expected cash flows."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice A is incorrect. This choice incorrectly states that contraction risk is related to the increase in the expected life of a mortgage pool due to falling interest rates and higher prepayment rates. In reality, falling interest rates and higher prepayment rates lead to a decrease in the expected life of a mortgage pool, which is known as contraction risk."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice B is incorrect. This choice inaccurately describes contraction risk as being associated with an increase in the expected life of a mortgage pool due to increasing interest rates and lower prepayment rates. However, these conditions actually result in extension risk, not contraction risk."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice D is incorrect. This option wrongly suggests that contraction risk involves a decrease in the expected life of a mortgage pool due to increasing interest rates and lower prepayment rates. These circumstances would actually cause extension risk rather than contraction risk."}],"remember":["Extension risk is the risk related to the increase in the expected life of a mortgage pool due to increasing interest rates and lower prepayment rates.","Prepayment risk is the risk that borrowers will prepay their mortgages earlier than expected, impacting the cash flows of mortgage-backed securities.","Mortgage-backed securities (MBS) are investment products that are backed by a pool of mortgages, where investors receive payments based on the interest and principal payments from the underlying mortgages.","Investors in MBS need to consider various risks such as interest rate risk, credit risk, and prepayment risk in addition to contraction risk."]},{"id":"936","page":436,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"A planned amortization class tranche (PAC)."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"A collateralized mortgage obligation (CMO)."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"A strip."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"A mortgage back security (MBS)."}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"GrossHaus Investment Bank is one of the largest German investment banks that has more than 38% market share in financing residential and commercial properties. GrossHaus is not only involved in the mortgage business, but it also issues securities to its investors against mortgage pools. Robin Frazer has recently purchased a security from a bank that gives him a claim to the principal portion of a mortgage payment on a mortgage pool. Which of the following securities has Frazer has purchased from the bank?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"A strip is a type of security that allows an investor to separately purchase the principal portion and interest portion of the mortgage payments on a mortgage pool. In the context of the question, Robin Frazer has purchased a principal-only strip (PO) from GrossHaus Investment Bank. These PO strips are typically sold at a discount and increase in size over time as the principal component of the mortgage grows. This is in contrast to interest-only (IO) strips, which decrease in size over time as the principal due on the mortgage decreases. Therefore, the security that Frazer has purchased is a strip, making choice C the correct answer."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice A is incorrect. A planned amortization class tranche (PAC) is a type of mortgage-backed security that is designed to protect investors from prepayment risk. It does not entitle the investor to the principal portion of a mortgage payment on a mortgage pool, but rather provides scheduled payments over a period of time."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice B is incorrect. A collateralized mortgage obligation (CMO) is a type of security that pools together mortgages and then issues tranches to investors based on the risk profile and cash flow needs. While it does involve mortgages, it doesn't specifically entitle an investor to the principal portion of a single mortgage payment."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice D is incorrect. A Mortgage Backed Security (MBS) represents an ownership interest in mortgage loans made by financial institutions. It does not specifically provide entitlement to the principal portion alone as it includes both principal and interest payments from homeowners."}],"remember":["Principal-only (PO) strips allow investors to separately purchase the principal portion of mortgage payments.","Interest-only (IO) strips allow investors to separately purchase the interest portion of mortgage payments.","Collateralized Mortgage Obligations (CMOs) pool together mortgages and issue tranches based on risk profiles.","Prepayment risk is a concern for investors in mortgage-backed securities.","Mortgage-backed securities (MBS) represent ownership interests in mortgage loans."]},{"id":"937","page":437,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Binomial model approach."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Best guess approach."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Monte Carlo simulation."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"Black-Scholes Model."}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Since the mortgage borrowers have the option to prepay the underlying securities at any time, the valuation of the mortgage securities with this embedded options is not possible with the traditional valuation model. One of the mortgage securities valuation model uses probability distributions to value securities. In other words, it values the securities by allocating different probabilities to the multiple variables like future interest rate, shape of the yield curve, default rate, prepayment rate, etc. Which of the following models uses this approach?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The Monte Carlo simulation is a computational algorithm that relies on repeated random sampling to obtain numerical results. In the context of mortgage-backed securities (MBS), the Monte Carlo simulation is used to value these complex financial instruments. The model does this by assigning different probabilities to various factors that can affect the value of the MBS. These factors include future interest rates, the shape of the yield curve, default rates, prepayment rates, recovery rates, and interest rate volatility. By considering these variables and their associated probabilities, the Monte Carlo simulation generates a range of possible outcomes. The average of these outcomes is then taken as the estimated value of the MBS. This approach allows for a more nuanced and accurate valuation of MBS, taking into account the embedded prepayment option and the various factors that can influence the value of these securities."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice A is incorrect. The Binomial model approach is a method used for the valuation of options and other financial instruments. It constructs a recombining binomial lattice (tree) for the short rate, from which one can calculate the price of fixed income securities. However, it does not assign probabilities to various factors such as future interest rates, yield curve shape, default rates, prepayment rates etc., which are crucial in valuing mortgage-backed securities. Choice B is incorrect. The Best guess approach relies on subjective judgment rather than probabilistic modeling to estimate values and risks associated with financial instruments. This approach lacks the systematic and comprehensive analysis provided by probabilistic models like Monte Carlo simulation in assessing various factors affecting MBS valuation. Choice D is incorrect. The Black-Scholes Model is used primarily for pricing European options and does not incorporate multiple risk factors or their probabilities as required in MBS valuation. It assumes markets are efficient with no transaction costs or taxes; these assumptions may not hold true in real-world scenarios involving mortgage-backed securities."}],"remember":["Mortgage-backed securities (MBS) are financial instruments that represent an ownership interest in a pool of mortgage loans.","Prepayment risk is a key consideration when valuing MBS, as borrowers can choose to prepay their mortgages early, impacting the cash flows to MBS investors.","Monte Carlo simulation is a powerful tool for valuing complex securities by considering multiple variables and their associated probabilities.","Factors such as future interest rates, yield curve shape, default rates, prepayment rates, recovery rates, and interest rate volatility all play a role in MBS valuation.","The Binomial model approach is commonly used for valuing options and constructing binomial lattices, but it does not assign probabilities to various factors like Monte Carlo simulation.","The Best guess approach relies on subjective judgment rather than probabilistic modeling, which may not provide a comprehensive analysis for valuing MBS.","The Black-Scholes Model is primarily used for pricing European options and may not be suitable for valuing MBS due to its assumptions about market efficiency."]},{"id":"3456","page":439,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"$1.65 million"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"$1.25 million"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"$2.04 million"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"$1.63 million"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Suppose that an investor owns a pass-through security with an initial principal of $500 million. The remaining mortgage balance at the beginning of a certain month is $400 million. Assuming that the SMM is 0.4125% and the scheduled principal payment is $5 million, the estimated prepayment for the month is:"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Note: We only take into account the remaining mortgage balance, not the initial amount of principal."},{"type":"p","text":"P repayment f or month i(in $) = SMM(beginning balance– scheduled principal repaymen"},{"type":"pre","text":"= 0.4125%($400m − $5m) = $1, 629, 375"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3457","page":440,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"$225,000"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"$22,500,000"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"$800,000"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"$200,500"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A fund holds & a 10 million nominal of the GNMA 5.5% 30-year bond with a face value of 100. It enters into a one-month dollar roll with a repo dealer bank in which it sells the security at a price of 100-08 and buys it back at a forward price of par. Assuming that the security experiences a 2% paydown (scheduled principal plus prepayments) during the term of the trade, estimate the value of the drop."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Drop is the price difference between the front month, i.e., month of sale, and the back month, i.e., the month of purchase. The bonds are sold for 100-08, hence proceeds = $10m(100 + 8/32)/100 = $10, 025, 000 Purchasing the security at par in the back month, bearing in mind that the security has experienced a 2% paydown, will cost:"},{"type":"pre","text":"    $10m × (1– 2%) × (100/100) = $9, 800, 000\nDrop = $10, 025, 000 − $9, 800, 000 = $225, 000"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3458","page":440,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"CPR = 0.2141%; SMM = 0.01786%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"CPR = 4.8%; SMM = 0.409%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"CPR = 5.1%; SMM = 0.4353%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"CPR = 3.4%; SMM = 0.2878%"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Consider a pool of mortgages that were issued exactly 16 months ago at an effective interest rate of 6% p.a(they are beginning the 17th month). What is the CPR, and what is the SMM assuming 150 PSA?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Assuming 100 PSA CPR(month t | t ≤ 30) =6% × t/30 CPR(month 17) = 6% × 16/30 = 3.2%"},{"type":"p","text":"150 PSA implies that CPR (at the beginning of month 17) = 1.5 × 3.2% = 4.8% SMM = 1– (1– CP R)1/12 = 1– (1– 0.048)1/12 = 0.409%"},{"type":"p","text":"Note: The Public Securities Association model prepayment benchmark is one of the models used to estimate the monthly rate of prepayment. It is based on the assumption that rather than remaining constant, the monthly repayment rate gradually increases as a mortgage pool ages. The model assumes that: (I) CPR = 0.2% for the first month after origination, increasing by 0.2% every month up to 30 months (II) CPR = 6% for months 30 to 360"}],"remember":["Conditional Prepayment Rate (CPR) is the annualized percentage of the principal of a pool of mortgages that is expected to prepay in a year, given a certain set of assumptions.","Single Monthly Mortality (SMM) is the percentage of the remaining principal balance of a pool of mortgages that is expected to prepay in a month.","PSA (Public Securities Association) is a prepayment benchmark used to measure prepayment speeds for mortgage-backed securities.","The PSA model assumes a gradual increase in prepayment rates over time, starting at 0.2% and increasing by 0.2% each month up to 30 months, then remaining constant at 6% from month 30 to 360.","Prepayment models are essential in valuing mortgage-backed securities and assessing the risks associated with prepayment behavior."]},{"id":"3459","page":441,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"$436,000"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"$13,045"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"$109,680"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"$97,698"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"TBA prices of the Freddie Mac 5% for June 10 and July settlements are $104.20 and $103.00, respectively. The accrued interest to be added to each of these prices is $0.160. The expected total principal paydown (scheduled principal plus prepayments) is 2% of the outstanding balance and the prevailing short-term rate is 1.5%. Also, assume that the actual/360- day convention is applied. An investor wishes to roll a balance of $10 million. Determine the value of the roll."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Proceeds from selling the June 10 TBA are: $10m × (104.20 + 0.160)/100 = $10, 436, 000 Investing these proceeds to July 10 at 1.5% interest earns interest of: $10, 436, 000 × 30/360 × 1.5% = $13, 045 Purchasing the July 10 TBA, which has experienced a 2% paydown, will cost: $10m × (1– 2%) × (103.0 + 0.160)/100 = $10, 109, 680 Net proceeds from the roll therefore are: $10, 436, 000 + $13,045 − $10, 109, 680 = $339,365 If the investor does not roll, the net proceeds are the coupon plus principal paydown: $10m × (5%/12 + 2%) = $241, 667 Value of the roll = net proceeds from the roll – net proceeds without roll: = $339, 365 − $241,667 = $97, 698"}],"remember":["TBA (To Be Announced) prices are used in the mortgage-backed securities market to represent a generic MBS for future delivery.","Accrued interest is the interest that has accumulated on a bond since the last interest payment date.","Principal paydown refers to the reduction in the outstanding balance of a loan or security due to scheduled principal payments or prepayments.","The prevailing short-term rate is the current interest rate in the short-term market, which can impact investment decisions.","The actual/360-day convention is a day-count convention where the actual number of days in a month and year are used for interest calculations."]},{"id":"3460","page":442,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"4.5%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"5.1%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"4.9%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"4.0%"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A mortgage-backed portfolio includes four mortgage investments as follows:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Mortgage 1: $140,000 in current value, 5% interest rate, 5 years remaining duration\nMortgage 2: $100,000 in current value, 4% interest rate, 6 years remaining duration\nMortgage 3: $50,000 in current value, 6% interest rate, 3 years remaining duration\nMortgage 4: $60,000 in current value, 3% interest rate, 2 years remaining duration"},{"type":"p","text":"What is the weighted average coupon of the portfolio?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The weighted average coupon (WAC) is the weighted-average interest rate of mortgages that underlie a mortgage-backed security (MBS) at the time the securities were issued. It represents the average interest rate of a pool of mortgages with varying interest rates. Total value of portfolio = $140,000 + $100,000 + $50,000 + $60,000 = $350,000 We then compute the percentage value of each mortgage:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Mortgage 1 %value = $140,000/$350,000 = 40%\nMortgage 2 %value = $100,000/$350,000 = 28.6%\nMortgage 3 %value = $50,000/$350,000 = 14.3%\nMortgage 4 %value = $60,000/$350,000 = 17.1%"},{"type":"p","text":"The percentage values of each mortgage are then multiplied by their respective interest rates:"},{"type":"pre","text":"40% * 5% = 2%\n28.6% * 4% = 1.1%\n14.3% * 6% = 0.9%\n17.1% * 3% = 0.5%"},{"type":"p","text":"The resulting figures are then totaled to produce a WAC of approx. 4.5%."}],"remember":["Weighted Average Coupon (WAC) is a crucial metric in analyzing mortgage-backed securities (MBS).","WAC is calculated by taking the weighted average of the interest rates of the underlying mortgages in a portfolio.","It helps investors assess the overall interest rate risk and potential returns of the MBS.","Understanding the WAC is essential for risk management and investment decision-making in the fixed income market.","Investors should also consider factors like prepayment risk, credit risk, and market conditions when evaluating MBS investments."]},{"id":"3461","page":444,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"4.4"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"5.1"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"4.9"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"4.0"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A mortgage-backed portfolio includes four mortgage investments as follows:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Mortgage 1: $150,000 in current value, 5% interest rate, 5 years remaining duration\nMortgage 2: $100,000 in current value, 6% interest rate, 6 years remaining duration\nMortgage 3: $50,000 in current value, 4% interest rate, 3 years remaining duration\nMortgage 4: $80,000 in current value, 7% interest rate, 2 years remaining duration"},{"type":"p","text":"What is the weighted average maturity (n years) of the portfolio?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Weighted average maturity (WAM) is the weighted average amount of time until the maturities on mortgages in an MBS. Total value of portfolio = $150,000 + $100,000 + $50,000 + $80,000 = $380,000 We then compute the percentage value of each mortgage:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Mortgage 1 %value = $150,000/$380,000 = 39.5%\nMortgage 2 %value = $100,000/$380,000 = 26.3%\nMortgage 3 %value = $50,000/$380,000 = 13.2%\nMortgage 4 %value = $80,000/$380,000 = 21%"},{"type":"p","text":"The percentage values of each mortgage are then multiplied by the remaining duration until maturity:"},{"type":"pre","text":"39.5% * 5 years = 1.975 years\n26.3% * 6 years = 1.578 years\n13.2% * 3 years = 0.396 years\n21% * 2 years = 0.42 years"},{"type":"p","text":"The resulting figures are then totaled to produce a WAM of approx. 4.4 years"}],"remember":["Weighted average maturity (WAM) is a measure used in fixed income analysis to calculate the average time until a portfolio's securities mature, weighted by the proportion of the portfolio each security represents.","WAM is an important metric for investors in mortgage-backed securities (MBS) as it helps in assessing the interest rate risk associated with the portfolio.","Duration is another key concept in fixed income analysis, representing the sensitivity of a bond's price to changes in interest rates.","Weighted average duration (WAD) is another metric that combines the concept of duration with the weighting of each security in the portfolio.","Investors often use WAM and WAD to manage their exposure to interest rate risk and make informed investment decisions."]},{"id":"3463","page":446,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"$5,520.00"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"$5,426.21"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"$5,225.50"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"4,834.55"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Consider the following residential mortgage:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Loan amount: $500,000\nAnnual rate of interest: 5.5% (fixed)\nTerm: 10 years\nStart date: 01/01/2019"},{"type":"p","text":"If payments are made monthly, what is the amortized amount for the 10-year loan?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"A fixed-rate mortgage is a mortgage loan that has a fixed interest rate for the entire term of the loan. Equal payments are made over the life of the mortgage."},{"type":"pre","text":"PMT =                                Prin ci pal\n                                     ( 1−(1r+r)−n)"},{"type":"p","text":"Where: P MT = amortized amount for each month"},{"type":"p","text":"r = monthly interest rate (annual rate/12)"},{"type":"p","text":"n = total number of months"},{"type":"p","text":"r = 0.055/12 = 0.004583, n = 10 ∗ 12 = 120"},{"type":"pre","text":"PMT =        500, 000                                    = $5, 426.21\n       (  1  −(1 +0.004583                       )−1)20\n                 0.004583"},{"type":"p","text":"On a financial calculator, n = 120; I /Y = 0.4583 (5.5/12); P V = -500,000; F V = 0; CP T ⇒ PMT = $5,426.21"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3464","page":447,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"$150"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"$149.33"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"$500"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"$750"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A 30-year $150,000 mortgage has a fixed mortgage rate of 6 percent compounded monthly. What portion of the first payment is the principal?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"A fixed-rate mortgage is a mortgage loan that has a fixed interest rate for the entire term of the loan. Equal payments are made over the life of the mortgage."},{"type":"pre","text":"PMT =           Prin ci pal\n                ( 1−(1r+r)−n)"},{"type":"p","text":"Where: P MT = amortized amount for each month"},{"type":"p","text":"r = monthly interest rate (annual rate/12)"},{"type":"p","text":"n = total number of months"},{"type":"p","text":"r = 0.06/12 = 0.005,n = 30 ∗ 12 = 360"},{"type":"pre","text":"PMT =           150, 000        = $899.33\n       ( 1−(10+.00.00505)−36)0"},{"type":"p","text":"On a financial calculator, N = 360; I /Y = 0.5 % (6/12%);"},{"type":"p","text":"P V = -150,000; F V = 0; CP T ⇒ PMT = $899.33 The interest portion for a month is equal to the mortgage balance at the beginning of the month ($150,000 in this case) multiplied by the interest rate per month. Interest portion = 0.005 * 150,000 = $750 The remaining portion of the payment, $899.33 - $750 = $149.33, goes to reduce the principal balance."}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3465","page":448,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"$180,000"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"$220,000"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"$6,313"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"$218,245"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Consider a 15-year $500,000 mortgage with a 6 percent interest rate. After 10 years, the borrower (the mortgage issuer) pays off the outstanding principal. How much will the lender receive?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"We first need to know the monthly payment, PMT."},{"type":"pre","text":"PMT =                                       Principal\n                                            (  1  −(1 +r  )−n)\n                                                      r"},{"type":"p","text":"Where: PMT = amortized amount for each month"},{"type":"p","text":"r = monthly interest rate (annual rate/12) n = total number of months r = 0.06/12 = 0.005,n = 15 ∗ 12 = 180"},{"type":"pre","text":"PMT =                                  500, 000                 = $4, 219.28\n                                       ( 1−(10+.00.00505)−18)0"},{"type":"p","text":"On a financial calculator,"},{"type":"p","text":"n = 180;"},{"type":"p","text":"I/Y = 0.5 (6/12); (when using the financial calculator, we input the rates in percentage form. In this case, 0.005 =0.5%)"},{"type":"p","text":"PV = -500,000;"},{"type":"p","text":"FV = 0;"},{"type":"p","text":"CPT ⇒ PMT = $4,219.28"},{"type":"p","text":"From this point, there are two ways to go: First. We could construct an amortization table for the mortgage, but this would consume a lot of time. But there’s a simpler approach. After 10 years, we can treat this mortgage as though it were a 5-year mortgage with payments of $4,219.28 and an interest rate of 6 percent. We can then solve for the outstanding principal balance using the same formula."},{"type":"pre","text":"                   Outstanding Principal\n4, 219.28 =\n                        ( 1−(10+.000.0505)−6)0"},{"type":"p","text":"Solving for the outstanding principal gets us $218,245"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3467","page":449,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"$265"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"$101"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"$0"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"$111"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Consider a 15-year $500,000 mortgage with a rate of 6 percent. Ten years into the mortgage, rates have fallen to 5 percent. What would be the monthly saving to a homeowner from refinancing the outstanding mortgage balance at the lower rate? (Round your answer to the nearest unit.)"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"We first need to know the monthly payment, PMT."},{"type":"p","text":"PMT = Principal"},{"type":"pre","text":"(  1−(1+r   )−n)\n         r"},{"type":"p","text":"Where: PMT = amortized amount for each month r = monthly interest rate (annual rate/12)"},{"type":"p","text":"n = total number of months"},{"type":"p","text":"r = 0.06/12 = 0.005,n = 15 ∗ 12 = 180"},{"type":"pre","text":"PMT =            500, 000\n                                    = $4, 219.28\n              ( 1−(1+0.00.00505)−18)0"},{"type":"p","text":"On a financial calculator, n = 180;"},{"type":"p","text":"I /Y = 0.5 (6/12); ; (not 0.005, because it's already in percentage format) P V = -500,000;"},{"type":"p","text":"F V = 0;"},{"type":"p","text":"CPT ⇒ PMT = $4,219.28 From this point, there are two ways to go: First. We could construct an amortization table for the mortgage, but this would consume a lot of time. But there’s a simpler approach. After 10 years, we can treat this mortgage as though it were a 5-year mortgage with payments of $4,219.28 and an interest rate of 6 percent. We can then solve for the mortgage balance using the same formula."},{"type":"pre","text":"4,219.28 =        Principal\n                ( 1−(10+.000.0505)−6)0"},{"type":"p","text":"Solving for the principal gets us $218,245 For comparability, we calculate the new payments assuming a loan of $218,245, a 5-year life, and a rate of 5 percent."},{"type":"pre","text":"PMT =         218, 245               = $4, 118.59\n           (  1−(1+0.004167   )−)60\n                    0.004167"},{"type":"p","text":"Thus, the saving is approx."},{"type":"p","text":"$101 per month($4, 219.28 − $4, 118.59 = $100.69. Note: it would be misleading to compare the payments on the old loan to a new, 15-year loan."}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3585","page":451,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"remains constant for the first 30 months and then increases by 0.2% for months 30 to 360"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"increases by 0.2% every month up to 30 months"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"decreases by 0.2% every month up to 30 months"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"remains constant for the first 30 months and then decreases by 0.2% for months 30 to 360"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"The Public Securities Association (PSA) prepayment benchmark assumes that the monthly prepayment rate for a mortgage pool:"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The Public Securities Association (PSA) prepayment benchmark assumes that the monthly prepayment rate for a mortgage pool increases as it ages (becomes seasoned). The PSA is expressed as a monthly series of Conditional Prepayment Rates (CPRs). The model assumes that:"},{"type":"pre","text":"CPR = 0.2% for the first month after origination, increasing by 0.2% every month up to\n30 months; and\nCPR = 6% for months 30 to 360"},{"type":"p","text":"A mortgage pool whose prepayment speed (experience) is in line with the assumptions of the PSA model is said to be 100% PSA. Similarly, a pool whose prepayment experience is two times the CPR under the PSA model is said to be 200% PSA (or 200 PSA)."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice A is incorrect. The PSA prepayment benchmark does not assume that the monthly prepayment rate remains constant for the first 30 months and then increases by 0.2% for months 30 to 360. Instead, it assumes that the rate increases by 0.2% every month up to the first 30 months."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice C is incorrect. The PSA model does not assume a decrease in the monthly prepayment rate over time, but rather an increase of 0.2% each month until it reaches a certain point (usually around month 30)."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice D is incorrect. Similar to choice A, this option incorrectly assumes that the monthly prepayment rate remains constant for an initial period and then decreases afterwards, which contradicts with how PSA model works."}],"remember":["Conditional Prepayment Rate (CPR): The percentage of the remaining principal balance that is prepaid in a single period, typically a month.","Public Securities Association (PSA): A prepayment benchmark used in the mortgage-backed securities market to standardize prepayment assumptions.","Seasoning: Refers to the age of a mortgage pool, with prepayment rates typically increasing as the pool ages.","100% PSA: Indicates that the prepayment speed of a mortgage pool aligns with the assumptions of the PSA model.","200% PSA: Indicates that the prepayment speed of a mortgage pool is twice the CPR under the PSA model."]},{"id":"3586","page":452,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Accelerate prepayments and reduce the average life of the MBS"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Decelerate prepayments and increase the average life of the MBS"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Accelerate prepayments and increase the average life of the MBS"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"Decelerate prepayments, but and decreases the average life of the MBS"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"During periods of falling interest rates, the refinancing of mortgage loans will:"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"During periods of falling interest rates, homeowners are more likely to refinance their mortgage loans. Refinancing allows homeowners to replace their existing mortgage with a new one that has a lower interest rate. This process accelerates prepayments because homeowners are paying off their old mortgage faster than originally planned. Prepayments are the early return of principal on a mortgage security. When prepayments accelerate, the average life of the mortgage-backed security (MBS) reduces. The average life of an MBS is the weighted-average time to the return of a dollar of principal, measured in years. When homeowners prepay their mortgages, the principal is returned sooner, reducing the average life of the MBS."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice B is incorrect. Falling interest rates typically encourage borrowers to refinance their mortgage loans, which accelerates prepayments. This in turn reduces the average life of the MBS, not increase it as this option suggests."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice C is incorrect. While it's true that falling interest rates accelerate prepayments due to increased refinancing activity, this actually reduces the average life of the MBS rather than increasing it. The faster prepayment speed means that investors receive their principal back sooner than expected, thus shortening the average life of the security. Choice D is incorrect. This choice incorrectly suggests that falling interest rates decelerate prepayments and decrease the average life of MBS. In reality, lower interest rates lead to an acceleration in prepayments due to increased refinancing activity by borrowers and consequently reduce (not decrease) the average life of MBS."}],"remember":["Prepayments: Early return of principal on a mortgage security Weighted-average life of an MBS: The average time to the return of a dollar of principal, measured in years Refinancing: Process of replacing an existing mortgage with a new one that has a lower interest rate Interest rates: Falling interest rates typically lead to increased refinancing activity by homeowners Accelerated prepayments: Occur when homeowners pay off their mortgages faster than originally planned"]},{"id":"3587","page":453,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Time to maturity as a percentage of the total life of the security"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Remaining principal of the ith mortgage to the total principal"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Absolute value of coupon payments due"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"Remaining number of months to maturity for each mortgage loan"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Which of the following are considered as weights while determining the weighted average maturity of a mortgage pass-through security?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The weighted average maturity (WAM) of a mortgage pass-through security is calculated by"},{"type":"p","text":"assigning weights to the remaining life of each mortgage in the pool. The weight assigned to each mortgage, denoted as wi, is determined by the ratio of the remaining principal of the ith mortgage (Pi) to the total remaining principal of all mortgages in the pool. This is mathematically represented as: wi = Pi/ ∑ni=1 Pi. This method of weighting ensures that mortgages with larger remaining principals have a greater impact on the WAM, reflecting the fact that these mortgages will take longer to be fully repaid and thus have a greater effect on the average maturity of the security."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice A is incorrect. The time to maturity as a percentage of the total life of the security is not used as weights in calculating WAM. Instead, it's the remaining principal balance of each mortgage that is used."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice C is incorrect. The absolute value of coupon payments due does not serve as weights for calculating WAM. This would be more relevant in determining cash flows rather than the average time it will take for mortgages to be repaid."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice D is incorrect. While the remaining number of months to maturity for each mortgage loan does play a role in determining WAM, it's not used as weights in its calculation. Rather, these are multiplied by their respective weights (i.e., remaining principal balances) to arrive at WAM."}],"remember":["Weighted Average Maturity (WAM) is a measure used in the analysis of mortgage-backed securities to determine the average time until the principal is expected to be repaid.","WAM helps investors assess the interest rate risk and prepayment risk associated with mortgage pass-through securities.","Prepayment risk refers to the risk that borrowers will repay their mortgages earlier than expected, impacting the cash flows to investors.","Factors such as interest rates, economic conditions, and borrower behavior can influence prepayment risk in mortgage-backed securities."]}]);
